The **platformOS Agent Playbook** is one part of the [platformOS Agent Kit](/developer-guide/ai/ai): the part that lives in your project rather than on a server.

The [MCP servers](/developer-guide/ai/mcp-servers) give an agent the ability to look platformOS up and to act on an instance. The Playbook is what tells it which facts matter, in what order to work, and what it must not do. It unpacks to a single directory in your project root — `.claude/`, `.opencode/`, `.pi/` or `.codex/`, depending on the tool.

## What is in it

The paths below are the Claude Code layout; the other tools' equivalents are in [One Playbook, four wirings](#one-playbook-four-wirings).

| Path | What it holds |
|---|---|
| `CLAUDE.md` | The order of work and the prohibitions, loaded on every request |
| `rules/` | Eight per-directory rule files, loaded when the agent opens a file in that directory — pages, partials, layouts, commands, queries, GraphQL, schema and translations — plus two that always load |
| `knowledge/` | Fourteen topic files, loaded on demand: Liquid syntax, filters, tags and data; GraphQL reads and writes; requests and context; deploy and operations; translations; YAML and schema; verifying and debugging |
| `agents/platformos-coding-agent.md` | The platformOS coding agent — see below |
| `hooks/`, `diagnostics.json` | A hook that attaches the documented platformOS rule, and its fix, to a validation finding |
| `settings.json` | Makes the coding agent the default, wires up the hook, and sets a status line showing the model and environment in use |

## The platformOS coding agent

`agents/platformos-coding-agent.md` defines a named agent whose job is writing and changing platformOS code — Liquid, GraphQL, YAML schema, translations and deploys. `settings.json` makes it the **default agent for the project**, so it handles platformOS work without you having to select it.

Why a dedicated agent rather than rules alone: a general-purpose agent treats "write the file" as the task and verification as optional. This one carries the platform's working order inside its own definition, so the order survives even when the request is a one-liner.

## The order of work

`CLAUDE.md` states one sequence, and the prohibitions exist to protect it:

1. **Look it up.** Any tag, filter, object, GraphQL operation or type the agent is not sure of, through the Liquid and GraphQL knowledge servers.
2. **Validate the buffer.** `validate_code`, with the content about to be written, and every file of one change in a single call so they resolve against each other.
3. **Write the files.**
4. **Preview, then deploy.** A dry run first, because a non-partial deploy removes everything live that the project does not contain.
5. **Make a real request.** Fetching the page is the only thing that proves it works.
6. **Read the data back.** A mutation can return an error payload and leave the record untouched.

The rule it spends the most words on is the one hardest to resist: **writing a file and deploying it to find out whether it works is prohibited.** That puts unvalidated code on an instance, and a deploy is not a test.

## What it asserts, and why so little

The Playbook is deliberately narrow. A fact earns a place in it only when it **fails silently** — no error, no warning, no failed deploy — *and* no tool in the toolchain catches it. Anything a linter already reports does not need to occupy an agent's attention.

What is left is the set of platformOS behaviours that look like working code:

- Everything lives under `context.` — a bare `{% raw %}{{ product }}{% endraw %}` renders empty and never raises.
- `{% raw %}{% graphql %}{% endraw %}` must appear above every use of its result; below, the result renders blank with no error.
- `record_delete` needs `table`, or the delete fails and the record survives.
- Array and hash filters return a new value; discarding the result leaves the original untouched.
- A filter in a tag *argument* is silently dropped and still deploys.
- `{% raw %}{% response_status 404 %}{% endraw %}` sets a status; assigning `context.statusCode` renders fine and still answers `200`.
- A missing translation key renders `translation missing: en.x.y` as visible page copy, with a `200`.
- Nothing checks record ownership: a by-id update or delete will happily write to another user's row.
- CSRF is not enforced for you — an unverified POST still runs, anonymously.

## Install it

Answer yes when [`pos-cli ai init`](/get-started/ai-assisted-development#set-up-your-ai-tool) offers it, or pass the flag:

<pre class="command-line" data-user="user" data-host="host"><code class="language-bash">
pos-cli ai init --tool claude --agent-playbook
pos-cli ai init --tool opencode --agent-playbook
pos-cli ai init --tool pi --agent-playbook
pos-cli ai init --tool codex --agent-playbook
</code></pre>

By hand, which is also how you update it:

<pre class="command-line" data-user="user" data-host="host"><code class="language-bash">
curl -fsSL https://documentation.platformos.com/assets/platformos-agent-playbook.zip -o playbook.zip
unzip -o playbook.zip &amp;&amp; rm playbook.zip
</code></pre>

For the other tools, fetch the archive named for it from the same place: `platformos-agent-playbook-opencode.zip`, `platformos-agent-playbook-pi.zip` or `platformos-agent-playbook-codex.zip`.

Each archive unpacks straight to its own directory, with no nested directory to flatten. `-o` overwrites without asking, so it replaces every Playbook file **including any you have edited**; drop the `-o` and unzip elsewhere if you want to diff first.

## One Playbook, four wirings

All four archives carry the same platformOS knowledge, compiled from the same source. What differs is how that knowledge reaches the agent, because the tools provide different mechanisms.

| | Claude Code | opencode | pi | Codex |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Installs into | `.claude/` | `.opencode/` | `.pi/` | `.codex/` |
| Agent definition | `agents/` + `settings.json` | `agents/` + `default_agent` | `APPEND_SYSTEM.md` | `AGENTS.md` |
| Always-on rules | `CLAUDE.md` | the `instructions` key | `APPEND_SYSTEM.md` | `AGENTS.md` |
| Per-directory rules | native: a rule's `paths` field | the Playbook's plugin | the Playbook's extension | an index to read from |
| Diagnostics on a finding | a `PostToolUse` hook | the same plugin | the same extension | — |

The per-directory rules are the interesting difference, and the reason there are four columns rather than one.

Claude Code loads a rule file only when you open a file its `paths` field matches, so a rule about pages arrives when a page is opened and costs nothing otherwise. **opencode and pi have no equivalent**, so their archives ship a small plugin that reimplements it: it watches which file each tool call touched, matches it against each rule's `globs`, and delivers the rule once per session. The same plugin explains a `validate_code` finding from the same table the Claude Code hook reads. Both plugins share one delivery module, so the two cannot drift apart, and neither has any dependencies.

**Codex has no lazy mechanism at all.** It builds its instruction chain once at startup, from the project root down to your working directory, and a nested `AGENTS.md` is read only when Codex is *started* in that directory — not when a file there is touched. Its whole chain is also capped at 32 KiB, which it truncates silently. So the Codex archive ships its rules as files with an index in `AGENTS.md` naming which to read before working in a given directory. That relies on the agent following the index rather than on the harness, which is the honest trade for what Codex provides.

Cursor, VS Code and anything else that speaks MCP get the whole rest of the Agent Kit — the same four servers and the same tools. Only the Playbook needs a per-tool layout.

{% include 'alert/important', content: 'An existing `.claude/`, `.opencode/`, `.pi/` or `.codex/` directory is never replaced, and neither is an existing `AGENTS.md`. They hold your own rules, settings and any edits you have made, and unpacking over them would overwrite them. `pos-cli ai init` leaves them alone and prints the download link so you can merge in the parts you want.' %}

## Two tools where the Playbook can be installed and inert

Both pi and Codex gate a project directory behind trust, and neither reports an error when the gate is closed — the install succeeds, the session starts, and none of the Playbook is read.

- **pi** ignores everything in `.pi/` until the project is trusted. Start pi in the project and accept its prompt, or set `"defaultProjectTrust": "always"` in your pi settings.
- **Codex** ignores a project `.codex/` layer until the project is listed as trusted in your *global* configuration, and ignores hooks until they are separately reviewed. `pos-cli ai init` offers to add the trust entry for you, and prints the exact block if you decline.

`pos-cli ai init` reports both of these after installing, because nothing about the install itself would look wrong.

